! ================================================================================
! CISCO PACKET TRACER CONFIGURATION & EXPERIMENT: PROBLEM 4
! Interconnection of LAN-A (Hub) and LAN-B (Switch) via Crossover Cable
! Analysis of Multi-MAC Port Learning and Extended Broadcast Domains
! ================================================================================

! --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
! 1. TOPOLOGY & PHYSICAL INTERCONNECTION
! --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
! LAN-A consists of Hub-A with 3 hosts:
!   - Host-A1 (Fa0 -> Hub-A Fa0)
!   - Host-A2 (Fa0 -> Hub-A Fa1)
!   - Host-A3 (Fa0 -> Hub-A Fa2)
!
! LAN-B consists of Cisco 2950 Switch-B with 3 hosts:
!   - Host-B1 (Fa0 -> Switch-B Fa0/1)
!   - Host-B2 (Fa0 -> Switch-B Fa0/2)
!   - Host-B3 (Fa0 -> Switch-B Fa0/3)
!
! INTER-LAN TRUNK/UPLINK:
!   Hub-A Port FastEthernet3 <========================> Switch-B Port FastEthernet0/4
!                  [Copper Cross-Over Cable]

! --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
! 2. UNIFIED IP ADDRESSING SCHEME (Subnet 192.168.1.0/24)
! --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
! LAN-A End Hosts:
!   Host-A1: IP 192.168.1.1, Mask 255.255.255.0, MAC: 0002.16A1.0001
!   Host-A2: IP 192.168.1.2, Mask 255.255.255.0, MAC: 0002.16A2.0002
!   Host-A3: IP 192.168.1.3, Mask 255.255.255.0, MAC: 0002.16A3.0003
!
! LAN-B End Hosts:
!   Host-B1: IP 192.168.1.4, Mask 255.255.255.0, MAC: 0060.2F11.0001
!   Host-B2: IP 192.168.1.5, Mask 255.255.255.0, MAC: 0060.2F22.0002
!   Host-B3: IP 192.168.1.6, Mask 255.255.255.0, MAC: 0060.2F33.0003

! --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
! 3. SWITCH-B CONFIGURATION (Cisco IOS)
! --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
enable
configure terminal
hostname Switch-B

interface FastEthernet0/4
 description Interconnect Uplink to Hub-A
 switchport mode access
 spanning-tree portfast
 no shutdown
 exit

end
write memory

! --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
! 4. BEFORE CROSS-LAN PINGS: RECORD INITIAL TABLES
! --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
! [Host-A1 / Host-B1 Command Prompt]
! > arp -d   (Flushed ARP tables)
! > arp -a
! No ARP Entries Found.
!
! [Switch-B CLI]
! Switch-B# clear mac-address-table dynamic
! Switch-B# show mac-address-table
!           Mac Address Table
! -------------------------------------------
! Vlan    Mac Address       Type        Ports
! ----    -----------       --------    -----
! Total Mac Addresses for this module: 0

! --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
! 5. EXECUTE PINGS BETWEEN EACH PAIR OF HOSTS (LAN-A <-> LAN-B)
! --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
! [Cross-Ping 1: Host-A1 -> Host-B1]
! Host-A1> ping 192.168.1.4
! Reply from 192.168.1.4: bytes=32 time<1ms TTL=128
!
! [Cross-Ping 2: Host-A1 -> Host-B2]
! Host-A1> ping 192.168.1.5
! Reply from 192.168.1.5: bytes=32 time<1ms TTL=128
!
! [Cross-Ping 3: Host-A1 -> Host-B3]
! Host-A1> ping 192.168.1.6
! Reply from 192.168.1.6: bytes=32 time<1ms TTL=128
!
! [Cross-Ping 4: Host-A2 -> Host-B1]
! Host-A2> ping 192.168.1.4
! Reply from 192.168.1.4: bytes=32 time<1ms TTL=128
!
! [Cross-Ping 5: Host-A2 -> Host-B2]
! Host-A2> ping 192.168.1.5
! Reply from 192.168.1.5: bytes=32 time<1ms TTL=128
!
! [Cross-Ping 6: Host-A2 -> Host-B3]
! Host-A2> ping 192.168.1.6
! Reply from 192.168.1.6: bytes=32 time<1ms TTL=128
!
! [Cross-Ping 7: Host-A3 -> Host-B1]
! Host-A3> ping 192.168.1.4
! Reply from 192.168.1.4: bytes=32 time<1ms TTL=128
!
! [Cross-Ping 8: Host-A3 -> Host-B2]
! Host-A3> ping 192.168.1.5
! Reply from 192.168.1.5: bytes=32 time<1ms TTL=128
!
! [Cross-Ping 9: Host-A3 -> Host-B3]
! Host-A3> ping 192.168.1.6
! Reply from 192.168.1.6: bytes=32 time<1ms TTL=128

! --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
! 6. AFTER PINGS: RECORD UPDATED ARP & MAC FORWARDING TABLES
! --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
! [Host-A1 Command Prompt]
! > arp -a
!   Internet Address      Physical Address      Type
!   192.168.1.4           0060.2f11.0001        dynamic
!   192.168.1.5           0060.2f22.0002        dynamic
!   192.168.1.6           0060.2f33.0003        dynamic
!
! [Host-B1 Command Prompt]
! > arp -a
!   Internet Address      Physical Address      Type
!   192.168.1.1           0002.16a1.0001        dynamic
!   192.168.1.2           0002.16a2.0002        dynamic
!   192.168.1.3           0002.16a3.0003        dynamic
!
! [Switch-B CLI - THE CRUCIAL OBSERVATION]
! Switch-B# show mac-address-table
!           Mac Address Table
! -------------------------------------------
! Vlan    Mac Address       Type        Ports
! ----    -----------       --------    -----
! 1       0060.2f11.0001    DYNAMIC     Fa0/1
! 1       0060.2f22.0002    DYNAMIC     Fa0/2
! 1       0060.2f33.0003    DYNAMIC     Fa0/3
! 1       0002.16a1.0001    DYNAMIC     Fa0/4
! 1       0002.16a2.0002    DYNAMIC     Fa0/4
! 1       0002.16a3.0003    DYNAMIC     Fa0/4
! Total Mac Addresses for this module: 6

! --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
! 7. ARCHITECTURAL & PEDAGOGICAL INSIGHTS:
! --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
! 1. Multi-MAC Entry on a Single Switch Port:
!    Ports Fa0/1, Fa0/2, Fa0/3 connect to single host endpoints, so each port
!    learns exactly 1 MAC address.
!    In contrast, Port Fa0/4 connects to Hub-A. Since Hub-A repeats frames from
!    all three hosts in LAN-A, frames with source MAC addresses 0002.16a1.0001,
!    0002.16a2.0002, and 0002.16a3.0003 all arrive through port Fa0/4.
!    Consequently, Switch-B correctly associates THREE distinct MAC addresses
!    with the single physical port Fa0/4!
!
! 2. Broadcast Domain Fusion:
!    Connecting Hub-A to Switch-B fuses the two physical segments into a single
!    broadcast domain. An ARP broadcast initiated by any host is repeated by
!    the Hub and flooded by the Switch across all 6 connected nodes.
! ================================================================================

